theory.intro:
A **box plot** (or box-and-whisker plot) shows the five-number summary of a dataset drawn along a scaled number line.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\usetikzlibrary{arrows.meta}
\draw[{Stealth}-{Stealth}] (-0.4,0) -- (9.4,0);
\foreach \x in {0,...,9} {\draw (\x,-0.12) -- (\x,0.12);}
\node[below] at (4.5,-0.3) {number line in the data's units};
\draw[thick] (0.5,2.2) -- (2.6,2.2);
\draw[thick] (6.2,2.2) -- (8.6,2.2);
\draw[thick] (0.5,1.9) -- (0.5,2.5);
\draw[thick] (8.6,1.9) -- (8.6,2.5);
\draw[thick] (2.6,1.7) rectangle (6.2,2.7);
\draw[thick] (4.0,1.7) -- (4.0,2.7);
\node[above] at (1.55,2.8) {Whisker};
\node[above] at (5.1,2.8) {Box};
\node[above] at (7.4,2.8) {Whisker};
\node[above] at (2.6,1.0) {$Q_1$};
\node[above] at (4.0,1.0) {$Q_2$};
\node[above] at (6.2,1.0) {$Q_3$};
\node[above] at (0.5,0.35) {Minimum};
\node[above] at (4.0,0.35) {Median};
\node[above] at (8.6,0.35) {Maximum};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

